        <section class="source-introduction">

            <div class="reading-shell">

                <p class="section-label">
                    Before the destination
                </p>

                <h2>
                    The river began before I knew where it was going.
                </h2>

                <p class="source-opening">
                    I could not have imagined the life that was waiting for me.
                </p>

                <p>
                    At the time, there was only the day in front of me: another
                    game, another song, another challenge, another story unfolding
                    inside my head.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Looking backward, I can see that the qualities shaping my life
                    were already there. Curiosity. Imagination. Competition.
                    Independence. Loneliness. Discipline. The desire to understand
                    people and the need to become capable enough to survive whatever
                    came next.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The Source exists to preserve those waters—not so I can remain
                    in the past, but so I can understand what the past carried into
                    the present.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <article class="source-chapter source-chapter-light" id="chapter-one">

            <div class="chapter-shell">

                <header class="chapter-header">

                    <p class="chapter-number">
                        Chapter One
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        The Boy Who Lived in Stories
                    </h2>

                    <p class="chapter-subtitle">
                        Imagination, music, competition, and the quiet world
                        beneath the surface.
                    </p>

                </header>


                <div class="chapter-body">

                    <p class="chapter-opening">
                        Every river begins long before anyone notices it.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Mine began in places no one else could see.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        From the outside, I was a smart kid people wanted around.
                        I was liked, welcomed, and loved. But sometimes I wonder
                        whether being wanted kept me from being truly known.
                        People enjoyed my presence, yet very few entered the world
                        that existed inside my head.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        There was always another world.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Paperclips, pennies, straw wrappers, and whatever small
                        objects I could find became characters, tools, vehicles,
                        armies, and pieces of stories that could occupy me for
                        hours. I imagined my dog picking me up from school so I
                        could ride him home. I pictured myself exploring unfamiliar
                        places or becoming part of some enormous plot already in
                        motion.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I did not simply enjoy stories. I wanted to live inside them.
                    </p>

                    <blockquote class="source-quote">
                        Give me a story that reached far enough into me, and I was sold.
                    </blockquote>

                    <p>
                        Video games and movies became more than entertainment.
                        They were worlds filled with emotion, possibility, danger,
                        beauty, and meaning. I was fascinated by people too—what
                        they thought, why they behaved the way they did, and what
                        invisible reasons sat beneath the choices they made.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Much of home life was simply my mother and me. Holidays and
                        family events took us to Fort Worth, where food covered the
                        tables and adults filled the rooms with conversation.
                        I often drifted away from the center of it.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I would walk the land, swing from the rope swing, play with
                        the other children, pull pranks, explore, or wait for someone
                        to announce that an adventure was beginning. If anyone was
                        going somewhere, I wanted to go.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        The first music I remember
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        One of my earliest memories of music is my aunt Misty playing
                        “Hey There Delilah” and “She Will Be Loved.”
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        My mother played worship music often because church was a
                        major part of her life. I did not connect with much of it
                        then. But whenever she played Spanish music, something in me
                        came alive. I would listen and dance, even though I never
                        thought to seek the music out on my own.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Later, my brother introduced me to bands such as 3OH!3 and
                        Panic! at the Disco. Friends and culture eventually led me
                        toward rap, but alternative music and mainstream pop had
                        already become part of me.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        In fifth grade, school placed a recorder in my hands and
                        taught us simple songs. Before that, I had spent years
                        playing <em>The Legend of Zelda</em>, listening to the notes
                        carried through its world on an ocarina.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Those melodies fascinated me long before I understood why.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Then, in sixth grade, I began playing violin.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I continued until after high school.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Music became another language for thoughts and emotions I
                        did not always know how to explain.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        Competition without moderation
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        I was intensely competitive.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        At the Boys &amp; Girls Club, in Boy Scouts, at youth group,
                        in sports, or playing battle games with friends, I wanted
                        to win. Whether it was pool, ping-pong, dodgeball, a fire
                        contest, baseball, or a match of <em>Call of Duty</em>,
                        I gave everything I had.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        In baseball, I swung for a home run every time.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        When someone told me to run, I ran until I was completely
                        winded. I did not understand pacing myself. Even when I
                        watched other people conserve their energy, I could not
                        comprehend why they were not giving everything available
                        to them.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        In high school, I lost a varsity challenge to a player two
                        years older than me. He was good, and he made sure I knew it.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For the following year and throughout the summer, I practiced
                        for eight to twelve hours a day with one primary goal:
                        beat him.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        When tryouts returned, so did our match.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I dominated the first set. He crushed me in the second.
                        The final tiebreak became a four-hour battle that ended
                        twenty-four to twenty-two in my favor.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        No one could believe I had beaten him.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        But I had built an entire year around that moment.
                    </p>

                    <blockquote class="source-quote">
                        I was not merely trying to win. I was trying to prove that
                        obsession could overcome the distance between who I was and
                        who I wanted to become.
                    </blockquote>


                    <h3>
                        Surrounded, but often alone
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        Not everything came naturally.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I read more slowly than many of the children around me.
                        Sometimes during a lesson or a piece of music, I would lose
                        my place and become overwhelmed. Once I fell behind, it was
                        difficult to understand where everyone else had gone until
                        I could stop, reset, and find my place again.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Friendship was difficult in a different way.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I was an introvert who did not know how to enter a conversation
                        naturally. I struggled to put myself forward unless someone
                        directly asked what I thought.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        But when someone did ask, I could explain myself freely.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I could express ideas, defend my position, and speak without
                        fear. I simply did not know how to initiate closeness.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I spent eight years at the Boys &amp; Girls Club during the
                        school year and even longer stretches there during the
                        summers. I learned games, skills, and independence, but I
                        remained an individual player. I watched people closely,
                        listened to older friends, and absorbed advice from anyone
                        who seemed to understand something I did not.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Yet even then, I had a strong sense of discernment. I could
                        recognize when something felt false, and I rarely feared
                        confrontation when I believed something was wrong.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        My father was away. Loneliness became familiar. The future
                        remained impossible to picture.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I did not have a grand vision for adulthood.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I had the moment in front of me.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Looking back, I can see the river beginning—not in a single
                        dramatic event, but in hundreds of small streams.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A boy lost in imaginary worlds.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A musician captivated by beauty.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A competitor unable to give less than everything.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A quiet observer trying to understand people.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        At the time, I thought I was simply growing up.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I did not know the river had already begun to flow.
                    </p>

                </div>

            </div>

        </article>


        <section class="chapter-transition">

            <div class="reading-shell">

                <p class="section-label light-label">
                    The current deepens
                </p>

                <h2>
                    Imagination taught me to dream. Work taught me to endure.
                </h2>

                <p>
                    The next season would transform competition into discipline,
                    discipline into capability, and capability into a hunger for
                    independence.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


        <article class="source-chapter source-chapter-warm" id="chapter-two">

            <div class="chapter-shell">

                <header class="chapter-header">

                    <p class="chapter-number">
                        Chapter Two
                    </p>

                    <h2>
                        Learning to Build
                    </h2>

                    <p class="chapter-subtitle">
                        Work, independence, influence, and the neighborhoods
                        that taught me what I could endure.
                    </p>

                </header>


                <div class="chapter-body">

                    <p class="chapter-opening">
                        Before I learned how to sell, I learned how to work.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        My first job did not come with a paycheck.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        From roughly seven to twelve years old, I helped my mother
                        clean a barber shop. I dusted, cleaned sinks and toilets,
                        swept, mopped, buffed floors, emptied trash, and removed
                        piles of hair from the receptacles.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        My mother also taught me how to shine shoes.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I would finish the pairs she had for the day, and she would
                        inspect them closely. She showed me what I missed and made
                        me do the work with greater care.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I did not understand it then, but she was teaching me that
                        effort and excellence were not the same thing.
                    </p>

                    <blockquote class="source-quote">
                        Working hard was not enough. The work still had to be right.
                    </blockquote>


                    <h3>
                        The first paychecks
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        As I grew older and began playing sports, I earned my first
                        real paycheck at Rose Park Tennis Center in Abilene.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I worked behind the counter, accepted payments for concessions,
                        rackets, grips, and strings, and scheduled private and group
                        lessons. At church, alongside playing violin in the praise
                        team, I also helped at the counter accepting payments, though
                        that role was unpaid.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        My first formal job requiring employment paperwork was at
                        Carmike Theatres, which later became AMC Classic.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I worked floor staff, ran concessions, swept theaters,
                        cleaned, and restocked. It also gave me my first real friend
                        group beyond school.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The job came with free movies and snacks, along with partnerships
                        that gave employees free ice cream and access to a twenty-four-hour
                        gym.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        That was when bodybuilding entered my life.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Even as a teenager, my routines were harder, longer, and more
                        exhausting than those of many adults around me. The same
                        instinct that drove me in sports followed me into the gym.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        Too capable to remain where I was
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        Eventually, minimum wage began to feel like a ceiling.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        My friend Bubba suggested I cross the parking lot and apply
                        at Olive Garden. I hoped to become a waiter, but I was too
                        young to serve alcohol, so they hired me as a busser.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I worked relentlessly.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I was often among the first people in and the last people out.
                        After the kitchen staff and servers had gone home, I could
                        still be inside cleaning floors, walls, and anything else
                        that needed attention.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Some nights I left between midnight and two in the morning,
                        went directly to the gym, and then woke up for school.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Samuel Milchrest noticed.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        He told me I was too intelligent to remain a busser and moved
                        me immediately into a to-go specialist role.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I began taking phone orders, organizing catering packages,
                        assembling salads and dishes, checking every bag, remembering
                        drinks, straws, silverware, and two mints, and meeting customers
                        at the front to complete the handoff.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        It became my first sustained experience communicating with
                        the public.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        When I moved to College Station, I carried a strong recommendation
                        into another Olive Garden. They accepted me as a to-go specialist
                        and agreed to train me as a server when I turned eighteen.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Once I began serving, I excelled.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I wrote everything down, remembered special requests, spoke
                        easily with customers, and frequently received strong tips.
                        Serving revealed that the quiet child who struggled to begin
                        conversations could become confident when given a role,
                        a purpose, and a person directly in front of him.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        Discovering influence
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        By then, I already knew I could influence people.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I had persuaded friends and family to join plans, encouraged
                        restaurant guests to move beyond their usual choices, and
                        defended my decisions to teachers and adults.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        English literature taught me how to build persuasive arguments.
                        Stories, anime, and movies taught me that belief and force of
                        will could reshape how people interpreted the world around them.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        At the STAR Student banquet at Abilene Christian University,
                        I told the story of perseverance, my father being incarcerated,
                        and periods when my mother and I faced homelessness.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        People cried.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        That moment revealed something important: a true story,
                        honestly told, could move people.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Even as a small child, I had thought about what I would later
                        understand as the butterfly effect. One decision could affect
                        another person, whose decisions would affect someone else,
                        continuing outward until a seemingly insignificant action
                        changed circumstances far beyond my sight.
                    </p>

                    <blockquote class="source-quote">
                        I believed one life could create consequences across the world,
                        even if the person who began them never saw where they ended.
                    </blockquote>


                    <h3>
                        The invitation into another life
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        While attending Texas A&amp;M, I encountered a recruiting
                        table inside the Memorial Student Center.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        That was where I met Luke, Braedon, Rob, and the team recruiting
                        for Vivint.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        They challenged the assumption that college alone would give
                        me practical skills for creating income and navigating the
                        real world.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Their message reached the part of me already craving independence.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Door-to-door sales appeared to offer something larger than a job.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        It offered freedom.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I imagined being able to enter any city, speak to strangers,
                        create value, and earn money without depending on a traditional
                        boss or fixed schedule.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Luke brought me to my first Vivint sale in Houston. The homeowner
                        was Charlie Rivago.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I watched the system being installed: the panel, sensors,
                        glass-break detector, motion detector, thermostat, locks,
                        garage control, doorbell camera, and outdoor camera.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I watched Luke influence the conversation, call in a leader,
                        and close the deal.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Then I understood that I could learn the system and do it myself.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        It was on.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        The park bench
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        We moved to Ohio, and I was dropped into my first neighborhood.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I immediately realized I sounded scattered.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        In the center of the neighborhood, beside a pond, there was a
                        park bench. I sat there for the remainder of my first day.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The next day, I returned to the same bench.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I refused to continue until I had organized the pitch in a way
                        that made sense to me.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I needed a plan. I needed preparation. Once I had both, I was
                        ready to stand up and begin again.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        Thousands of doors
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        I made only thirty-three sales during my first summer.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        At one point, I went roughly a month and a half without selling
                        anything.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Still, I woke up, entered the neighborhoods, and knocked.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        There were days of nine to twelve hours on the doors. Saturdays
                        could stretch for twelve hours in a single neighborhood.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I had no car with me. Sometimes no food, water, or easy access
                        to a bathroom. I learned to ask strangers for help when I was
                        hungry or dehydrated.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Sometimes ten houses refused even a glass of water.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I sat on curbs soaked in sweat. I walked miles to gas stations.
                        I endured heat, rain, exhaustion, humiliation, and the basic
                        physical realities of being stranded outdoors all day.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        People cursed at me, threatened me, pointed guns, told me I
                        was worthless, and demanded that I find a real job.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Others simply said they were satisfied with what they had and
                        shut the door.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Sometimes those quiet rejections hurt more than the anger.
                    </p>

                    <blockquote class="source-quote">
                        Developing thick skin is not the absence of pain. It is learning
                        to feel the rejection and still walk to the next door.
                    </blockquote>

                    <p>
                        I learned that when I wanted to quit, I could pause briefly,
                        gather myself, and do one more door.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Then another.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Then another.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        Learning people from the doorway
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        Over time, neighborhoods became maps inside my mind.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I learned body language, positioning, timing, tone, preoccupation
                        breakers, and how to move someone from a distracted state into
                        a real conversation.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I learned to lead rather than merely react.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        At first, I could spend hours talking with someone about life,
                        children, marriage, work, or anything else, believing the
                        conversation was moving toward a sale. Then they would say
                        they needed to speak to a spouse or leave for an appointment.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I learned to interrupt respectfully, ask better questions,
                        guide the conversation, and protect the purpose of the interaction.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I also entered lives far beyond the sale.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I sat inside homes while couples argued about the security
                        system. I listened to strangers tell me about their marriages,
                        children, fears, and histories. Some customers became friends.
                        We ate together, shared drinks, smoked, and talked until I
                        nearly forgot I was there to sell anything.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Children and animals sometimes followed me through neighborhoods.
                        On difficult days, I found small victories in funny shapes in
                        trees, dogs perched in strange places, cats, rabbits, squirrels,
                        and anything that made the day feel briefly human.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        Building the salesman
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        Vivint surrounded us with instruction.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        We watched top representatives approach doors. We studied
                        products, listened to sales philosophy, consumed podcasts,
                        practiced pitches, and role-played in front of the entire office.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Some mornings, the anxiety and embarrassment were intense.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        But the repetition sharpened me.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The culture was built around competition: prizes, recognition,
                        trips, office status, and the pride of becoming one of the
                        people everyone watched.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        During my first year, I once sold six systems in a week.
                        In later seasons, I reached ten or eleven.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        In those moments, the office celebrated me. I felt like a
                        champion. Then, almost unconsciously, I often slowed down.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        It was as though receiving the emotional reward of success
                        convinced part of me that the work was already complete.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        There were also days of complete flow.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        On those days, the only thing capable of stopping me was the
                        sun going down. It felt as though anyone who opened the door
                        would eventually own a security system.
                    </p>


                    <h3>
                        Chasing freedom into captivity
                    </h3>

                    <p>
                        Outside the doors, I read self-improvement books, listened to
                        audiobooks, consumed motivational videos, trained in the gym,
                        and searched constantly for an advantage.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Some mornings, I took too much pre-workout, became sick, and
                        still spent the day knocking.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Drives to sales areas could last forty-five minutes or more
                        than two hours. I often slept in the vehicle and woke in a
                        neighborhood I had never seen, knowing it would become my
                        entire world for the day or the week.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I told myself I was sacrificing the present to create freedom
                        later.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I gave up time with my girlfriend, my dog, friends, childhood,
                        rest, and the ability to decide what I wanted from an ordinary
                        day.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The philosophy was simple:
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Sacrifice now. Become capable. Become successful. Enjoy the
                        rewards later.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        But later kept moving.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I ended up working more.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Even while producing, I remained a broke college student,
                        watching commissions disappear into deductions, back-end
                        structures, expenses, and systems I did not fully control.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The work made me resilient, persuasive, observant, adaptable,
                        and capable of entering almost any room or conversation.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        It also made loneliness deeper.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Most people could not understand an ordinary Tuesday spent
                        alone for eight hours among strangers, dropped into a place
                        I did not choose and expected to survive through my own will.
                    </p>

                    <blockquote class="source-quote">
                        I entered sales because I believed it would make me free.
                        Instead, it taught me how easily freedom can become another
                        thing we postpone.
                    </blockquote>

                    <p>
                        I had learned to work.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I had learned to influence.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I had learned to endure.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I was becoming extraordinarily capable.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I had not yet learned whether capability alone could build
                        a life worth living.
                    </p>

                </div>

            </div>

        </article>


